By the time I'm far enough into the game to have identified
the amulet in question, I'd usually be able to predict that
situation in advance -- and if the level allows teleport, I'd
be able to escape the crowd before my hitpoints got down
to half.  Non-teleport situations (e.g., Medusa's Titan) can
be more of a problem, granted; I wouldn't expect an amulet
of life-saving to solve that one.

But I've lost two or three amulets of life saving to simple
mistakes that I made, things that happened in one turn
because I didn't take a necessary precaution or tried to
do something I oughtn't have.  Again, maybe this is
because I'm relatively new to the game.

> So I ended up prefering better AC (which should mean
> shield),

I like to wear a shield for the AC when I'm moving
forward into unexplored or monster-intensive areas,
but I like to throw it in a blessed bag of holding (as
long as I can do so without losing reflection in the
process)  when making stash runs, price-ID runs,
altar runs, and whatnot back up through previously
cleared sections of the dungeon.  It gives me more
carrying capacity and allows me to make fewer trips,
and I can still get the shield out if I run into any real
nasties (say, if on my trip back to Minetown to see
the aligned priest again the RNG decides that a
random hill orc reading a cursed scroll of create
monster on DL8 should result in four titans or
some funky jazz like that).

> better attack (which means classes that
> can either dual wield or use attack spells
> drop the shield)

Sometimes I drop the shield even for major combat
if I can use a relatively good second weapon (e.g.,
if I've found a silver saber to combine with Excalibur),
provided I can get to at least Skilled in two-weapon
combat as well as both of the weapons involved.

> and better strategy (oh right, learn from my mistakes.
> I wish that worked for the classic "death by typo").

Indeed.  With most games, a typo just means you
have to go back to your last save.  That's annoying,
especially if there's no autosave or if you've done
something difficult recently, but at least your *whole*
game isn't trashed.

I understand why nethack doesn't do things that way:
a high degree of randomness is a critical part of how
the game is designed, and save/restore would really
compromise the RNG's power.  Nonetheless, it does
significantly exacerbate the typo problem.

> Reflection saves more of my characters from
