Granted, I *most* frequently die to short-term mistakes in
the early game, before I've so much as seen (much less
identified) any amulets.  Nonetheless, I've also had it
happen later in the game.

A couple of games ago on NAO I lost a very promising
knight at the castle just because I stepped on the
drawbridge before all the monsters beyond it were
dead (and one soldier had a wand of striking). I had
a decent number of hitpoints (for midgame) and good
AC and could easily have killed them all, and I'd even
wiped out the Ls and Rs, seen via telepathy, using
blessed genocide, before opening the bridge for the
first time.  I'd killed all the stuff in the moat as well.
Oh, and I had a pet yellow dragon.  I *could* have
killed all the remaining monsters in the castle, if I hadn't
been an idiot and stepped onto the bridge.  If nethack
had an "undo" button -- even if you could only use
it a very small number of times per game -- I'd
probably still be playing that game.  Sure, a more
experienced player probably wouldn't have made
the same mistake.  I'm new to the game, granted.
Does that mean I must always die and start over
from the VERY beginning, with nothing?  Okay, a
lot of the time that IS what I have to do, but if the
game will throw me a bone, in the form of an amulet,
that'll give me a chance to sidestep such a mistake
once and continue my game, I'm not going to turn
it down.  I've lost promising characters to simple,
one-turn mistakes post-castle, post-quest, and
even (once so far) after clearing a goodly portion
of Gehennom.  And that hurts a little, each time,
because I've invested so much effort into that
game to get that far.


